  "failure_diagnosis": {
    "cause_1_full_escalation_costs_more_than_going_straight_there": "a strategy pays for every rung it walks past, so reaching the reference rung by escalation costs 332,880 against 327,680 for going there directly. The policy escalated all the way on most scenarios, so its saving in the FAR band was outweighed",
    "cause_2_the_undecidable_stop_is_corrupted_by_the_bias_it_hunts": {
      "by_band": {
        "FAR": 0,
        "MODERATE": 0,
        "NEAR": 76,
        "UNDECIDABLE": 0
      },
      "early_stops_below_the_reference_rung": 76,
      "explanation": "the stopping rule declares a decision statistically undecidable when |mu_k - tau| falls inside the statistical band. But mu_k is the BIASED prediction. A biased rung can therefore push a perfectly decidable scenario to look like a coin flip, and the policy stops paying exactly when it should have carried on",
      "of_which_decided_wrongly": 36,
      "this_is_why_A5_failed": true
    },
    "cause_3_indicator_conservatism": {
      "consequence": "the successive-difference indicator over-estimates the finer rung's error, so the robustness test rarely passes at the medium rung and the policy escalates past a fidelity that was already sufficient. The preregistration declared this would happen in the MODERATE band; it did",
